WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:02.230
Hey Thoughty2 here.

00:00:02.230 --> 00:00:07.310
You may surprised to hear that a lot of things
you do that you think are perfectly innocent

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:12.820
may, in fact, be illegal.

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:16.670
1.

00:00:16.670 --> 00:00:22.000
Imagine this scene, you’re in the pub with
your mates watching the game, and you decide

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.370
to make a bet on the game’s outcome.

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:31.860
If you bet over $2,000 you just broke the
law and it can land you up to 10 years in

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:32.870
prison.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:40.020
This actually happened in 2005 when a cop
overheard a guy named Sal Culosi make a bet

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.860
of over $2,000 in a bar.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:48.250
The next morning a SWAT team arrived at his
doorstep to arrest him and shot him through

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.250
the heart.

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:50.250
2.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.760
Number 2 is connecting to unsecure WiFi networks.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:58.350
Connecting to any wireless network you don’t
own or have permission to use, even if it

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:04.410
isn’t password protected can land you in
prison for at least 2 years and you may have

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:09.080
to pay up to a $10,000 fine.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:14.460
Considering that most modern phones and wireless
devices automatically seek out and connect

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:19.580
to nearby WiFi networks makes this an extremely
easy law to break.

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:23.870
It’s extremely unlikely you’ll actually
be caught in the act, despite this there have

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:29.530
been at least 4 known cases in the US where
people have been arrested and locked up for

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.890
using other people’s WiFi.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:32.890
3.

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:37.380
Speaking of the internet, at number 3 is using
a fake name online.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:44.430
It may sound stupid but the next time you
signup to a website under the alias of SuperNinja66

00:01:44.430 --> 00:01:46.940
you’ve actually broken the law.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:52.070
It’s considered illegal under the Computer
Fraud and Abuse Act to fake your identity

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:56.740
online and can net you between 5 and 20 years
in prison.

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:57.740
4.

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:00.650
At 4 is writing disturbing material.

00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:05.729
Okay this doesn’t apply everywhere but in
some US states for example, Illinois, it is

00:02:05.729 --> 00:02:08.179
illegal to write “disturbing fiction”.

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:13.299
It doesn’t even matter if you publish it
or not, if someone finds disturbing material

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:19.500
that you’ve written and finds it offensive
you could be fined up to $1,500 and face up

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.920
to 30 days in jail.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:27.019
It’s even worse in Oklahoma, where you can
face up to 10 years in prison for writing

00:02:27.019 --> 00:02:31.469
a fictional story wherein a person gets injured
or killed.

00:02:31.469 --> 00:02:32.469
5.

00:02:32.469 --> 00:02:37.769
At 5 is a peculiar law you’ve probably broken
countless times in your life.

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:44.239
In the UK and certain US states, such as Alaska,
it is actually illegal to be drunk in a pub

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:45.709
or a bar.

00:02:45.709 --> 00:02:50.489
The law defines being drunk as having “lost
steady self-control”.

00:02:50.489 --> 00:02:55.650
Although very few people are ever prosecuted
for this, in practice the courts use markers

00:02:55.650 --> 00:03:02.779
such as glazed eyes, slurred speech and unsteadiness
to determine if one is properly badgered enough

00:03:02.779 --> 00:03:06.040
to face charges for being drunk in a pub.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.040
6.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:10.340
Number 6 is a law that you’ve probably contravened
more than any.

00:03:10.340 --> 00:03:14.409
It is illegal to sing Happy Birthday in public.

00:03:14.409 --> 00:03:15.409
Why?

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:16.930
Because it’s copyrighted.

00:03:16.930 --> 00:03:23.530
In 1988 Warner Chappell Music purchased the
rights to the song for $25 million dollars.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:27.650
Meaning restaurants that play the song have
to pay royalties to Warner.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:31.821
Collectively these royalties net Warner a
handsome $2 million dollars a year.

00:03:31.821 --> 00:03:39.180
In 1995 the American Society of Composers,
Authors and Publishers tried to extort money

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:46.299
from 2,300 girl scout groups because they
regularly sang Happy Birthday around the campfire

00:03:46.299 --> 00:03:48.669
without paying royalties.

00:03:48.669 --> 00:03:54.889
However the American press released a PR nightmare
upon ASCAP and they quickly backed down once

00:03:54.889 --> 00:03:58.549
they realised what utter tits they were being.

00:03:58.549 --> 00:03:59.549
7.

00:03:59.549 --> 00:04:05.909
And finally, in most of the US it’s illegal
to possess a permanent marker or aerosol can

00:04:05.909 --> 00:04:09.170
in public if you are under the age of 18.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:14.439
So if you ever took part in an arts and crafts
day as a child you’re technically a hardened

00:04:14.439 --> 00:04:15.669
criminal.

00:04:15.669 --> 00:04:22.060
There was a controversial case in 2010 when
a 13 year old Oklahoma City boy was arrested

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:25.380
for using a permanent marker in class.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.970
The teen was writing on a piece of paper using
a permanent marker and the ink had bled through

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.970
onto the desk underneath.

00:04:31.970 --> 00:04:37.600
His teacher actually made a citizens arrest
on the kid and he was handed over to the Police

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:38.600
and arrested.

00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:54.220
Thanks for the view, subscribe for more Thoughty2.

